n{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476623} {:answer/id #uuid "e2329fc9-a95d-5478-9111-cb7612f97e88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476567} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5583aa9-142f-5f88-ae4f-07e974bc5ce1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476851}] :question/id #uuid "b9c1cac7-603f-5a47-87fd-f2ee952aaad9" :question/math-academy-id "q-77955" :question/problem "The linear transformation $T$ has the standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nWhat are the respective images of the standard basis vectors $\\{i,j\\}$ of ${R}^{2}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nThe images of the vectors in the standard basis $\\{i,j\\}$ are the first and second columns of $T,$ respectively. \n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =T\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(j) & =T\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the images of the vectors in the standard basis $\\{i,j\\}$ by computing the matrix products $T\\cdot i$ and $T\\cdot j:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =T\\cdot i=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(j) & =T\\cdot j=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Images of the Standard Basis Vectors Under a Given Linear Transformation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133755 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133564 :question/id #uuid "0db64243-ffa4-595b-8555-5ee369450557" :question/math-academy-id "e-5437" :question/problem "In the diagram below, $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf j$ are the vectors of the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$, while $\\mathbf{i'}$ and $\\mathbf{j'}$ are their images under the linear transformation $\\mathbf T.$\nWhat is the standard matrix of $\\mathbf T?$\n\n![](images/4d/4d1b9e56c0c6214d949d12f45fe4aecea5516971203724ecf573a2b17c6e959a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Every linear transformation in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ can be represented as a $2\\times 2$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf j$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nFrom the diagram we see that\n\n- the image of $\\mathbf{i}$ is ![](images/95/95f30188b01782a32f4dbfdf48aae31e06846b0faa4a6f741e921ad64910221b.svg) and\n\n- the image of $\\mathbf{j}$ is ![](images/0f/0f695a9b9d87ca060eb5011b616d77b7e7d8d9394de6d537a2f2aab759c26a86.svg)\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is \n\n![](images/a2/a28158d8b163a3ec2ef4c568f7a4bbc63cf8b89385117a1e6178193d7cba886a.svg)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2845913-5c76-57f5-a4d3-139a6513225b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134426 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fd796e3-143b-5855-aea6-138f016b5e7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476273} {:answer/id #uuid "7e8d4d90-3061-5a98-a843-cf7ecb939b1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476483} {:answer/id #uuid "8776d28a-e6e4-5e00-bae7-b7f295