er/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392566} :answer-field/id #uuid "3447986d-3f82-5757-965c-a7760fd57f01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392562}] :question/id #uuid "3dc8d6e7-0a45-5ad2-ab8b-ad2aa66146a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-43892" :question/problem "Given that $\\{i,j\\}$ is the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ and that the images of $i$ and $j$ under the action of a linear transformation $T$ are $⟨1,4⟩$ and $⟨5,-1⟩$, respectively, what is the standard matrix of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Every linear transformation in ${R}^{2}$ can be represented as a $2\\times 2$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $i$ and $j$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for this linear transformation is \n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476928 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0470357e-6776-58dc-a92d-1244d3d2f1c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476212} {:answer/id #uuid "18af80ea-9a4f-54ab-a463-e12b9e6d6901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476245} {:answer/id #uuid "1b668640-c99e-560e-a7d5-c0c41236c343" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476257} {:answer/id #uuid "548d2944-fd2b-5335-9b9f-5e6beb5caf59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476381} {:answer/id #uuid "a6456ebd-14e4-53cb-9264-3b235bb3c519" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476245} :answer-field/id #uuid "69285345-cbe0-5515-804e-fe0f3c31e254" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476800}] :question/id #uuid "9d925f8f-8f71-5849-ad7a-d04b18e0b1d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-43832" :question/problem "Given that $\\{i,j\\}$ is the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ and that the images of $i$ and $j$ under the action of a linear transformation $T$ are $⟨1,-1⟩$ and $⟨2,3⟩$, respectively, what is the standard matrix of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Every linear transformation in ${R}^{2}$ can be represented as a $2\\times 2$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $i$ and $j$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for this linear transformation is \n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479862 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d4a0944-b188-5d35-baa8-8a8a88a994ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479406} {:answer/id #uuid "2ab529e4-93ab-5203-b0f9-a8a2a958fd1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479424} {:answer/id #uuid "958b422f-cfe6-5018-975b-a8efbacbddc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479584} {:answer/id #uuid "9cfa3692-efef-5b3e-940d-57c277809203" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479598} {:answer/id #uuid "f724ed95-68fb-54fb-b004-f6295840c4e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479584} :answer-field/id #uuid "cae2df7d-db8e-56df-a0a9-d18c8a2876b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479803}] :question/id #uuid "659a44e7-6c36-5b88-91e1-e77f6a2823f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-77952" :question/problem "Given that $\\{i,j,k\\}$ is the standard basis of ${R}^{3}$ and that the images of $i,$ $j,$ and $k$ under the action of a linear transformation $T$ are $⟨1,-4,-1⟩,$ $⟨-3,-2,5⟩,$ and $⟨1,0,0⟩,$ respectively, what is the standard matrix of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Every linear transformation in ${R}^{3}$ can be represented as a $3\\times 3$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $i,j,$ and $k$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is \n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479866 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28cc40d6-3384-53d9-a33b-2b15b315fb64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479422} {:answer/id #uuid "81c81ee7-5dc1-520e-b6bb-1d4571b8c42e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479551} {:answer/id #uuid "add2ffad-ea81-50