To find a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-2,$ we need to find a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A+2I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A+2I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A+2I)^{3}v=0.$ We're given that\n\n$$\n(A+2I)^{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,.\n$$\n\nThis means that *any* vector in ${R}^{3}$ is a solution to $(A+2I)^{3}v=0.$ Therefore, the solution to $(A+2I)^{3}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={v}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{v}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{v}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A+2I)^{2}v=0.$ We have\n\n$$\n(A+2I)^{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,.\n$$\n\nThis matrix is already in reduced row echelon form. This means that the solution to $(A+2I)^{2}v=0$ is ${v}_{3}=0,$ where ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are free variables. Therefore, the solution to $(A+2I)^{2}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}={v}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{v}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the solutions, we see that the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ satisfies $(A+2I)^{3}v=0$ but does *not* satisfy $(A+2I)^{2}v=0.$ \n\nHence, $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ of $A.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0e6a70d-57ec-5771-857e-7f76c85e2dcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398426} {:answer/id #uuid "4fb54a74-