can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={v}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{v}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{v}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A-9I)v=0.$ We have\n\n$$\nA-9I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,.\n$$\n\nThis matrix is already in reduced row echelon form. This means that the solution to $(A-9I)v=0$ is ${v}_{3}=0,$ where ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are free variables. Therefore, the solution to $(A-9I)v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}={v}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{v}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the solutions, we see that the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ satisfies $(A-9I)^{2}v=0$ but does *not* satisfy $(A-9I)v=0.$ \n\nHence, $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ of $A.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411193 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "440236b4-92aa-5254-905d-950abe8cffde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411167} {:answer/id #uuid "46495ace-b04a-59dd-8381-227a96c1c3fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411168} {:answer/id #uuid "7a216500-a2d0-5cdf-99d8-51869ef6a345" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411171} {:answer/id #uuid "b4cd298d-8652-556e-857d-083df393260d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bma