n form, we get\n\n$$\nA-6I∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the solution to $(A-6I)v=0$ is ${v}_{1}=0,$ where ${v}_{2}$ is a free variable. Therefore, the solution to $(A-6I)v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}={v}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing our two solutions, we see that the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ satisfies $(A-6I)^{2}v=0$ but does *not* satisfy $(A-6I)v=0.$  \n\nHence, $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ of $A.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397930 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aee402a8-e25f-5f7d-a299-4a23b73ed1f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397932} {:answer/id #uuid "ad9bfac8-1d7c-5077-8a13-0b233ce0e476" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397933} {:answer/id #uuid "dec6d280-d470-557d-a512-81311849870b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397934} {:answer/id #uuid "0a16bd0a-f467-5ad6-93c8-62261785fdf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397935} {:answer/id #uuid "d62f0927-56c7-51d7-8bad-0389d2905e20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397934} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8a307a7-86a5-5ea1-9e43-54f2220ef71e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397931}] :question/id #uuid "73f90d24-b39c-5f8a-bf8f-01fb835c9813" :question/math-academy-id "q-109739" :question/problem "Which of the following is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ for the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 5\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $k$ is a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)^{k}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-\\lambda I)^{k-1}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =5,$ we need to find a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-5I)^{2}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-5I)v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A-5I)^{2}v=0.$ First, we have\n\n$$\nA-5I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing its square, we see that this matrix is nilpotent:\n\n$$\n(A-5I)^{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \nThis means that *any* vector in ${R}^{2}$ is a solution to $(A-5I)^{2}v=0.$ Therefore, the solution to $(A-5I)^{2}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}={v}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{v}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A-5I)v=0.$ We have\n\n$$\nA-5I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,.\n$$\n\nConverting this matrix to reduced row echelon form, we get\n\n$$\nA-5I∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the solution to $(A-5I)v=0$ is ${v}_{1}=0,$ where ${v}_{2}$ is a free variable. Therefore, the solution to $(A-5I)v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}={v}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing our two solutions, we see that the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ satisfies $(A-5I)^{2}v=0$ but does *not* satisfy $(A-5I)v=0.$  \n\n Hence, $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ of $A.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492868 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0756e190-dd4c-5ab7-a236-7a5ff886e267" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492233} {:answer/id #uuid "2d242b8e-732d-5ce2-acc0-ac260e41c38d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492313} {:answer/id #uuid "377b0a2f-6aae-5a04-92b0-062f4e5e6c68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492334} {:answer/id #uuid "467f0506-3c1f-5339-bc87-b5b0503ffaea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492356} {:answer/id #uuid "5334fe9f-4786-52ef-9a98-9cb15e6b2c12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492375} :answer-field/id #uuid "3479556a-c757-58ee-a1e3-6c07d85dbb84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492725}] :question/id #uuid "65f11ed3-608e-50f9-bd7c-9bcf9678af5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-109723" :question/problem "Which of the following is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ for the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $k$ is a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)^{k}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-\\lambda I)^{k-1}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =1,$ we need to find a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-I)^{2}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-I)v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A-I)^{2}v=0.$ First, we have\n\n$$\nA-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing its square, we see that this matrix is nilpotent:\n\n$$\n(A-I)^{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis means that *any* vector in ${R}^{2}$ is a solution to $(A-I)^{2}v=0.$ Therefore, the solution to $(A-I)^{2}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}={v}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{v}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A-I)v=0.$ We have\n\n$$\nA-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,.\n$$\n\nConverting this matrix to reduced row echelon form, we get\n\n$$\nA-I∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the solution to $(A-I)v=0$ is ${v}_{1}=0,$ where ${v}_{2}$ is a free variable. Therefore, the solution to $(A-I)v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}={v}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing our two solutions, we see that the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ satisfies $(A-I)^{2}v=0$ but does *not* satisfy $(A-I)v=0.$   \n\nHence, $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ of $A.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492882 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "233af5f4-0a25-5bb3-b3b7-ffc87f6106eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492289} {:answer/id #uuid "81b8a399-ac1c-5b0c-9c61-401f6dbb06fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492480} {:answer/id #uuid "9eb4d4fb-b61b-536a-9c24-289877e466be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492527} {:answer/id #uuid "d2cced8e-5ebc-5468-a21f-cc66a9bd05eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492634} {:answer/id #uuid "ff648827-57dd-5515-ad21-7cc765d9fa24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492634} :answer-field/id #uuid "9df4b280-b0de-5f2d-8ef5-8defbec49836" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492766}] :question/id #uuid "8154c3ec-b3e2-5217-909c-bb51c90216b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-109701" :question/problem "Which of the following is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ for the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $k$ is a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)^{k}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-\\lambda I)^{k-1}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =2,$ we need to find a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-2I)^{2}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-2I)v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A-2I)^{2}v=0.$ First, we have\n\n$$\nA-2I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing its square, we see that this matrix is nilpotent:\n\n$$\n(A-2I)^{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis means that *any* vector in ${R}^{2}$ is a solution to $(A-2I)^{2}v=0.$ Therefore, the solution to $(A-2I)^{2}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}={v}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{v}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A-2I)v=0.$ We have\n\n$$\nA-2I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis matrix is already in reduced row echelon form. This means that the solution to $(A-2I)v=0$ is ${v}_{2}=0,$ where ${v}_{1}$ is a free variable. Therefore, the solution to $(A-2I)v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}={v}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing our two solutions, we see that the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ satisfies $(A-2I)^{2}v=0$ but does *not* satisfy $(A-2I)v=0.$  \n\nHence, $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is a generalized  eigenvector of rank $2$ of $A.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492903 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b5c5023-8317-5b6a-9a63-5d40c3212c13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492308} {:answer/id #uuid "3c922ed7-7ed8-5e11-8d35-4e2a8732c85c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492347} {:answer/id #uuid "7280c48f-4835-5c54-98b5-0b256f3ecbe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492443} {:answer/id #uuid "b9ec9dec-0e54-5246-ad74-fb6ffe7b5f85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492592} {:answer/id #uuid "e819ca14-0514-5ee6-8121-38f72b7729c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492347} :answer-field/id #uuid "2761b903-b51b-52f3-8ccc-b880239830d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492720}] :question/id #uuid "bc5bfa06-6c1e-57cc-be7f-ad1551c2f73d" :question/math-academy-id "q-109703" :question/problem "Which of the following is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ for the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $k$ is a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)^{k}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-\\lambda I)^{k-1}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =2,$ we need to find a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-2I)^{2}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-2I)v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A-2I)^{2}v=0.$ First, we have\n\n$$\nA-2I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing its square, we see that this matrix is nilpotent:\n\n$$\n(A-2I)^{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis means that *any* vector in ${R}^{2}$ is a solution to $(A-2I)^{2}v=0.$ Therefore, the solution to $(A-2I)^{2}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}={v}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{v}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A-2I)v=0.$ We have\n\n$$\nA-2I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nConverting this matrix to reduced row echelon form, we get\n\n$$\nA-2I∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the solution to $(A-2I)v=0$ is ${v}_{1}=0,$ where ${v}_{2}$ is a free variable. Therefore, the solution to $(A-2I)v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}={v}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing our two solutions, we see that the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ satisfies $(A-2I)^{2}v=0$ but does *not* satisfy $(A-2I)v=0.$  \n\nHence, $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ of $A.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560998 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02579b08-aa96-5657-8a37-f644aee46200" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560366} {:answer/id #uuid "066327c0-13e2-5e83-bb2a-0277f56ca7f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560374} {:answer/id #uuid "424a206b-d217-57c7-b985-5f98a09d4fbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560511} {:answer/id #uuid "dba8fc9b-1a1f-5317-aa3f-026b062a068d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560796} {:answer/id #uuid "f2f93766-83fb-5af9-bff4-e296409ac79e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560796} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7edbc4e-9dec-53a1-8adf-4c314e246c1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560949}] :question/id #uuid "51c69c9b-71bf-570d-be6e-a38ab651e0c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-109725" :question/problem "Which of the following is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7$ for the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $k$ is a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)^{k}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-\\lambda I)^{k-1}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =7,$ we need to find a vector $v$ that satisfies \n\n$$\n(A-7I)^{2}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-7I)v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A-7I)^{2}v=0.$ First, we have\n\n$$\nA-7I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing its square, we see that this matrix is nilpotent:\n\n$$\n(A-7I)^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis means that *any* vector in ${R}^{2}$ is a solution to $(A-7I)^{2}v=0.$ Therefore, the solution to $(A-7I)^{2}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A-7I)v=0.$ We have\n\n$$\nA-7I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$\n\nConverting this matrix to reduced row echelon form, we get\n\n$$\nA-7I∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the solution to $(A-7I)v=0$ is ${v}_{2}=0,$ where ${v}_{1}$ is a free variable. Therefore, the solution to $(A-7I)v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing our two solutions, we see that the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ satisfies $(A-7I)^{2}v=0$ but does *not* satisfy $(A-7I)v=0.$  \n\nHence, $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ of $A.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Generalized Eigenvector of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3634 :topic/title "Finding Generalized Eigenvectors of Specific Ranks"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116129} :activity/id #uuid "16b40f91-5658-5189-b1b3-5c72e2c31649" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116129 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116497 :tutorial/content "To compute the product of an elementary matrix and another matrix, one option is to perform the matrix multiplication as usual. However, there's an easier, quicker way.\n\nRemember that every elementary matrix is the result of applying an elementary row operation to the identity matrix. If we multiply another matrix by an elementary matrix, we're effectively applying the elementary row operation to the other matrix.\n\nFor example, suppose we are asked to find the product $EA$ where\n![](images/25/25d4c31649834fe3ae42a701d49f6e4a31fa8626de1d9aeed0f98ccbbf4a57fb.svg)\n\nThe elementary matrix $E$ comes from swapping the two rows of the identity matrix ($R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$). So, to compute the product $EA,$ all we have to do is swap the two rows of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/49/497b76accb211bbe4253ee2094206f70e8856f9510db71f6367f255fcc565665.svg)\n\nThis may seem almost too easy. But it's true! And we can verify it by computing the product the long way, using matrix multiplication. Indeed, we get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}EA & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we say that $E \\cdot A$ is equivalent to applying to $A$ the elementary row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2.$\n\n**Watch out!** It's important to multiply $A$ from the *left*. If we multiply $A$ by an elementary matrix from the *right*, we will perform a *column* operation on $A$ (not a row operation). In this case, multiplying $A$ by $E$ from the right will have the effect of switching the *columns* of $A,$ not the rows.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AE & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "527d3bb7-567f-5115-ad1c-31bd81fe1c23" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3166 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f7bbedb9-6a2a-55a3-b430-2e2720918232" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116130}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116130 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115246 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0f47800-98b3-512e-af20-1899e6b0c86d"} :step/id #uuid "7c86254d-65d6-53f5-80cb-058b8e3bc172" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116131}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116131 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115247 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94c2cd65-810b-52ad-a20d-7b5a0a9eaf2b"} :step/id #uuid "3eb5a2ce-3bcb-5884-a872-6bd15f8824da" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116132}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116132 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115248 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6f890b-fab1-51f1-9ac7-b680553c835a"} :step/id #uuid "5a0e9919-2a74-5681-a749-2ab1434ad705"}] :activity/title "Row Operations on 2x2 Matrices as Products of Elementary Matrices" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115473}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186048373 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115246 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115075 :question/id #uuid "51a437bf-2a11-5eba-a046-4770d41214ca" :question/math-academy-id "e-4720" :question/problem "Let ![](images/a9/a9f425a48a3ed8fabfce0eb1785ebdddc62d7dd3208ac302fd66a89b03e58cdb.svg) Find the row operation that, when applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ gives the result of the product $EA.$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $E$ is obtained by taking the identity matrix and replacing the second row with the result of adding $5$ times the first row to the second row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=5{R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $EA$ is equivalent to applying the row operation $R_2:=5R_1+ R_2$ to $A.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0f47800-98b3-512e-af20-1899e6b0c86d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115742 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3cee33d-b82e-55ac-9d59-fb0f6b715203" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-2)R_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113419} {:answer/id #uuid "d12ecaad-80d4-5fc0-8ae4-a3edc857b830" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R_{1}:=R_{1} + (-2)R_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113420} {:answer/id #uuid "e776709c-0d34-5fcc-986a-1310e6f94b24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R_{1}:=-2R_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113421} {:answer/id #uuid "e7971abe-a3ac-5311-a690-9440f11341dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R_{2}:=-2R_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113422} {:answer/id #uuid "487ccf96-b31f-539a-a92c-6b8bebd10ec9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R_{2}:=R_{2} + 2R_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113423}] :answer-field/id #uuid "88c14c4e-8097-5973-94bf-4d02e02c2822" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113418}] :question/id #uuid "6463483e-3cd6-514f-95b6-55e55248a2a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-32040" :question/problem "Let $E = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$. Find the row operation that, when applied to a $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix $A$, gives the result of the product $EA$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115846 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5e1154e-8614-5a40-8278-87626fddfb97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R_{2}:=R_{2} + 7R_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114014} {:answer/id #uuid "f22ffb35-4b33-56ec-806d-3632831778f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R_{1}:=R_{1} + 7R_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114015} {:answer/id #uuid "2bf6610f-79ab-5d5b-a9a7-e04dcfd3465f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R_{2}:=7R_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114016} {:answer/id #uuid "671abb1f-878d-5cbf-9e85-cfddedd59e3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-7)R_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114017} {:answer/id #uuid "7dbd8792-6f10-5330-803d-5a02a5c8fc4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R_{1}:=7R_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114018}] :answer-field/id #uuid "47f61f26-c5d5-5ff3-ba7a-1ad8246d633c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114013}] :question/id #uuid "af2485e6-cf6a-5823-8606-4537f7a8e4f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-42812" :question/problem "Let $E = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$. Find the row operation that, when applied to a $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix $A$, gives the result of the product $EA$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265983 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ef7082b-a8cb-5f1a-919c-08c1470cb74b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{1}+(-5){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263284} {:answer/id #uuid "26826c57-252e-56f3-928a-7ed1b119d8ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{1}+5{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263335} {:answer/id #uuid "81bc10fa-ce64-5073-9d1d-50f07fae01cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=-5{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/r