\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2}$ The  corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$ \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$ \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552525 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06d7d22f-6ff9-5b9a-82a9-bdfe6c4e8376" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551679} {:answer/id #uuid "5fb5dd7c-4b48-54a8-b83c-d7defd3de549" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551896} {:answer/id #uuid "c76bcf29-916c-5a10-b69c-1134c3821622" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552140} {:answer/id #uuid "cae2fb1b-9651-59ca-b1cd-ee01189afa5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552149} {:answer/id #uuid "e4466b78-f73e-5284-a533-d92c2c1c792b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552140} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d002de1-dbdf-5bab-854e-712d27f3c62c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552317}] :question/id #uuid "f5b55192-8108-5b5a-9d5a-dfeb97a067b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-75113" :question/problem "Suppose that the following elementary row operations, in the given order, are to be applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+4{R}_{2}\n$$\n\nFind the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication." :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the three given elementary row operations.\n\n- The first elementary row operation is ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third elementary row operation is ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+4{R}_{2}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+4{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, to apply the third elementary row operation to ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{3}$ on the left and get ${E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Matrix Product That's Equivalent To a Sequence of Row Operations"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1727 :topic/title "Row Operations on 2x2 Matrices as Products of Elementary Matrices"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125588} :activity/id #uuid "13665af8-c926-5261-b3fc-925c516a2fd1" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125565 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125849 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a stochastic matrix $P$ and a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}.$ Then, $\\mathbf{q}$ is a **steady-state vector** of $P$ if \n\n$$\nP\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{q}.\n$$\n\nFor example, to show that the stochastic vector \n![](images/ca/caba3c1b2f3245a3ebea0bd6ff6d47c63d409a7ba18a48db1f1269cb9ac13854.svg) \n\nis a steady-state vector for the stochastic matrix \n![](images/7f/7f231d09e7daf85eec90e682a9eb53c242aadac75b372e3685b65757ae3309dc.svg) \n\nwe compute the matrix product $P\\mathbf q\\mathbin:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =[\\begin{aligned}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.94+2\\cdot 0.03 \\\\ 0.06+2\\cdot 0.97\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =q\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $P\\mathbf q = \\mathbf q,$ the vector $\\mathbf q$ is a steady-state vector of $P.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e1601d67-32bc-5796-a800-6e704f25f991" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5500 :tutorial/title "Steady-State Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "965fe16f-9c57-5058-9e32-90332314977c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125566}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125566 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124615 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f2f4f4-af4a-597d-b6c6-afb82e708fc2"} :step/id #uuid "bd127c03-d025-5b21-ae93-e24b7aec916f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125567}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125567 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125850 :tutorial/content "To find a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P,$ we seek a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}$ such that \n\n$$\nP\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{q}.\n$$\n\nLet's begin by writing this equation in a slightly different form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector we seek is a solution to the matrix equation $(P-I)\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nLet's use this idea to find a steady-state vector for the matrix\n![](images/77/77ffe4078d3c62c30ab94b59bc9465a92db659b9e70b137bb144deb44844e800.svg)\n\nFirst, we compute $(P-I):$\n![](images/3d/3d91ece6103baa469569adf1994f3965cfc3429fbe65c0d7cfffff53b83b20ef.svg)\n\nThen, to solve $(P-I)\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{0},$ we reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.75 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0.75 & -0.5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-0.75 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\nx_1= \\dfrac 23 x_2.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n![](images/4a/4a5d81320f3e4eeee6b9fb09424238467d6c9e8b2e2246971ba3550b65a6d9c2.svg)\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, our steady-state vector is\n\n![](images/85/8515cb27fa64a5186fae0c63184df2ff7651c03963eef5804c4d6bedbace2283.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "78f635ff-e2e7-5c9f-9ec2-470187a45bc3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5501 :tutorial/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "429ea8df-33bc-5c79-a0d6-97b71166cd98" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125570}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125568 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124616 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f53fa522-6d50-51d9-abdd-62735575a2d1"} :step/id #uuid "8ba24a30-9b4e-5e58-93b1-b999bb45fe16" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125569}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125569 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124617 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84798595-cdd4-57bf-953f-0856b041b65f"} :step/id #uuid "3865f17c-09ee-5b73-96f9-45f1dd985dd9"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125570 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125851 :tutorial/content "A stochastic matrix $P$ is **regular** if there exists a positive integer $n$ such that $P^n$ has all positive elements.\n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- The stochastic matrix $P,$ given by\n![](images/e9/e9588bb9c77762d6caee5460403077eba65a891676f85a9e30ff99e7e6112184.svg) \nhas all positive elements. Therefore, it is a regular stochastic matrix.\n\n- The stochastic matrix $Q,$ given by\n![](images/e0/e0ba33618820d19e77d365a9f316239ef2e36133cbcac4007bf2b5c2f6e57137.svg) \ncontains a zero element. However, if we compute its square, we get\n![](images/29/29c5a411ac3c797699a39e35972e415e98df3347e66581bb5494f61a92d92bd5.svg) \nwhich has all positive elements. Therefore, $Q$ is a regular stochastic matrix.\n\nRegular stochastic matrices have the following important property:\n\n*Any regular stochastic matrix has a **unique** steady-state vector $\\mathbf q.$ Moreover, the Markov chain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1}, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =P{x}_{2}, \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮ \\\\ {x}_{k+1} & =P{x}_{k} \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nconverges to $\\mathbf q$ for **any** initial state $\\mathbf x_0.$*" :tutorial/id #uuid "255fadf0-9fc2-52d5-976b-015a97f52b08" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5502 :tutorial/title "Regular Stochastic Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "735b7b96-43fa-5c0e-b4b8-df7598a6610b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125568}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125588 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125855 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a population where people live either in the city or the countryside. A study carried out by the local authority shows that, each year,\n\n- $6\\%$ of the city population relocates to the countryside,\n\n- $3\\%$ of the countryside population relocates to the city, and\n\n- everyone else remains where they are.\n\nAdditionally, $70\\%$ of the population lived in the city and $30\\%$ in the countryside at the time of the study. \n\nWe can represent this situation using a stochastic matrix $P$ and initial state vector $\\mathbf x_0,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/44/44fa9ad32622bd1d7b3541206030ecc862a3db9420f46049c9f16992fdb947ca.svg) \n\nAs we've seen, to find the state vector $\\mathbf x_{k+1}$ that corresponds to the $(k+1)$th year, we left-multiply the state vector corresponding to the $k$th year by $P.$ This gives rise to the following Markov chain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1}, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =P{x}_{2}, \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮ \\\\ {x}_{k+1} & =P{x}_{k} \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe local authority wants to determine whether the state vectors settle down to a fixed state. With the aid of a computer, they compute the following states:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0.667 \\\\ 0.333\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0.636\\,97 \\\\ 0.363\\,03\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{3} & =P{x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0.609\\,64 \\\\ 0.390\\,36\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{100} & =P{x}_{99}=[\\begin{aligned}0.333\\,363 \\\\ 0.666\\,637\\end{aligned}]≈[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{101} & =P{x}_{100}=[\\begin{aligned}0.333\\,36 \\\\ 0.666\\,64\\end{aligned}]≈[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the data, we see that the vectors $\\mathbf x_{100}$ and $\\mathbf x_{101}$ are both approximately equal to the vector\n\n![](images/03/030d5719a268d9e15c2c58488ff1c36602d5c757475e79c25dcd1da5a4eb8712.svg)\n\nFurthermore, computing $\\mathbf x_{102}, \\mathbf{x}_{103}, \\ldots$ all give vectors approximately equal to $\\mathbf q.$ This suggests that the sequence $\\mathbf x_k$ converges to $\\mathbf q\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx_k \\rightarrow \\mathbf q \\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad k\\to\\infty.\n$$\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf q$ is called a **steady-state vector** of the matrix $P.$ Here, our steady-state vector suggests that after enough time has passed, one-third of the population will live in the city and two-thirds will live in the countryside.\n\nFinally, notice that for large values of $k,$ we have\n\n$$\nP\\mathbf x_k \\approx \\mathbf x_{k}.\n$$\n\nThis observation indicates how to find $\\mathbf q$ without resorting to computers." :tutorial/id #uuid "cb9435c4-44ed-58ec-9c04-b8fed4403fcd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6151 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7c429a3d-9dd8-5fe2-a7e7-cf0e9f515d1a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125565}}] :activity/title "Steady-State Vectors" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124712} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411620} :activity/id #uuid "86878afe-0434-571a-8f3d-da4de43c4751" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411618 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411615 :question/id #uuid "15361c81-aace-5a26-b7c5-2e82d38f451f"} :step/id #uuid "2f47d0d7-0048-532c-8ccd-1d7c100cde28" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411619}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411619 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411617 :question/id #uuid "fbc10db1-83ee-5f40-90d1-b581f73d6e92"} :step/id #uuid "a72fda11-1a51-5e4f-8796-9dbea4da037a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411620 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411616 :question/id #uuid "62944003-1237-5928-b405-2159dbe448f0"} :step/id #uuid "d2495fcf-fbe9-534c-8f3a-89d05a93dce9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411618}}] :activity/title "Steady-State Vectors" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411596}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186048393 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124615 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124426 :question/id #uuid "8914449a-3d6f-5847-9e94-3be0a2ccbc94" :question/math-academy-id "e-9178" :question/problem "![](images/3c/3ccabfea2a80fc5f858400c5bac362841b8c6f6b41cdc6fedbbfcd031d2241ca.svg)\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $\\mathbf{q}$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\mathbf{q}$ is a stochastic vector\n\nII. $P\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{q}$\n\nIII. $\\mathbf{q}$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}$ such that $P\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{q}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\mathbf{q}$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have \n![](images/e5/e51c398066c9be6bd62023005c888d115956938926a82f9d4695684f37421628.svg)\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $\\mathbf{q}$ is a stochastic vector such that $P\\mathbf{q}=\\mathbf{q}.$ So, it's a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f2f4f4-af4a-597d-b6c6-afb82e708fc2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124770 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e87c8124-0ff2-547b-8e5b-1dd24c33ff63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459395} {:answer/id #uuid "1db6c273-8e01-545f-91b6-8ebdebf4da20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459396} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff666d64-9efb-5fa3-a856-3094022b00fa" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460564} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3df9a2b8-8bba-539f-90aa-2cc738bd7714" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459397} {:answer/id #uuid "4ac0ac9d-8b8c-5d0a-9d3f-28652b7eb407" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459398} :answer-field/id #uuid "c610aaa1-fb31-5f5b-906e-c90bf52d5364" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460565} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "449c8856-7e56-53ff-9d15-616c80d5a4ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459399} {:answer/id #uuid "029706cb-7965-5a82-8339-455757b093e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459400} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba85054f-f2a2-5c15-b38d-4e5781404347" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460566}] :question/id #uuid "3662430d-84f0-5632-b066-9a7b273fafeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-110189" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{15} \\\\ \\frac{8}{15}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- **II.** $Pq=q$\n- **III.** $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have \n\n$$\nPq=\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{15} \\\\ \\frac{8}{15}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1.4}{15}+\\frac{5.6}{15} \\\\ \\frac{5.6}{15}+\\frac{2.4}{15}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{15} \\\\ \\frac{8}{15}\\end{bmatrix}=q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector such that $Pq=q.$ So, it's a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124771 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c39d6007-3d64-5685-b8c2-3bdda3ca55e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459401} {:answer/id #uuid "13c1dc0d-cdb6-5c69-8cf6-39d5bc66cef4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459402} :answer-field/id #uuid "959b7ae6-a403-5443-8e59-429047b0a0f0" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460567} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a223a2e-adf2-5b55-8492-76af71dae7f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459403} {:answer/id #uuid "5c89a81b-e4dc-5955-8a3f-a64c32b52cc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459403} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5f2897a-a72c-566d-a233-47a13942ed65" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460568} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0493696-0e98-587f-9bf7-c908798a7c68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459405} {:answer/id #uuid "e539bf2b-6167-5a95-961d-e33b0f253ec1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459405} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cd17c31-ac61-58e8-b271-80f4eeae1164" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460569}] :question/id #uuid "5d2ea1fa-42c3-5215-bede-8b745015a967" :question/math-academy-id "q-110239" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0.5 \\\\ 1 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- **II.** $Pq=q$\n- **III.** $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0.5 \\\\ 1 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0+\\frac{1.5}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1.5}{4}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ \\frac{5}{8}\\end{bmatrix}\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269024 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fab7f30-6daa-5568-b92a-c8222dbff730" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464024} {:answer/id #uuid "693c3e1