field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464043} :answer-field/id #uuid "db8add19-aac6-59b9-bd74-3fc72979db70" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464955} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70275795-38dd-5185-a8e8-361217a98984" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464044} {:answer/id #uuid "0025e570-a17e-5e5a-8fe9-a3f28e87701e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464044} :answer-field/id #uuid "60b6d1ea-dc1b-5ef2-bfa0-93caa5ee65f9" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464956} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f54bff3-c97e-5329-8c09-d1c4282670f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464046} {:answer/id #uuid "8aaafc5f-fc9b-57c8-8f39-25e81cc7060b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464046} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e30fd3b-e7f4-559e-b421-362f09cc1271" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464957}] :question/id #uuid "05952a75-ee49-5c6e-985c-55f5554bd3f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-135311" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.3 & 0.15 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.7 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.15 & 0.05\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 \\\\ 0.7 \\\\ 0.2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- **II.** $Pq=q$\n- **III.** $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=\\begin{bmatrix}0.3 & 0.15 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.7 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.15 & 0.05\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 \\\\ 0.7 \\\\ 0.2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.03+0.105+0.12 \\\\ 0.06+0.49+0.07 \\\\ 0.01+0.105+0.01\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.255 \\\\ 0.62 \\\\ 0.125\\end{bmatrix}\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411616 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16370931-5fc9-5536-9fd7-45afe61a10b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472688} {:answer/id #uuid "8ab6d543-ae19-5030-b18a-58051110b60a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472689} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b4b12e7-31db-52dd-a206-759d60508923" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473739} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71dd4b86-fcee-58b2-a783-19a83e01d1dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472690} {:answer/id #uuid "3f7a9115-7513-51f1-a218-6359bc259d69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472690} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dc16e75-61f9-56a2-8f15-331022147565" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473740} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3605e075-25c5-54da-bf16-7e2307304916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472692} {:answer/id #uuid "9db4a1e3-8985-5443-8d1e-4853b29ea3d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472692} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb71628d-05cf-5ce1-8a2b-c44a3882f058" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473741}] :question/id #uuid "62944003-1237-5928-b405-2159dbe448f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-135308" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.3 & 0.8 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.2\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 \\\\ 0.9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- **II.** $Pq=q$\n- **III.** $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=\\begin{bmatrix}0.3 & 0.8 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 \\\\ 0.9\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.03+0.72 \\\\ 0.07+0.18\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.75 \\\\ 0.25\\end{bmatrix}\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555828 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e737225-dfe8-56fa-9c5c-420bb05fb213" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555055} {:answer/id #uuid "3f79f79a-7208-5b41-a4f9-47134b6724e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555137} {:answer/id #uuid "6ccc323e-1621-5748-ad4b-61796a0013ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555249} {:answer/id #uuid "c96da41e-20e2-5525-b2cf-a83b365cb071" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555486} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf20109-e5a0-5d3e-879b-f5b09bf29f0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555249} :answer-field/id #uuid "30b36195-3069-564b-b4ee-eb8a18d3068c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555662}] :question/id #uuid "81d21d06-3e96-5b9a-927f-4e61cf10e9c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-135312" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.7 & 0.25 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.1 & 0.65 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.2 & 0.1\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{17}{38} \\\\ \\frac{11}{38} \\\\ \\frac{5}{19}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $q$ is a stochastic vector\n\nII. $Pq=q$\n\nIII. $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have \n\n$$\nPq=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.7 & 0.25 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.1 & 0.65 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.2 & 0.1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{17}{38} \\\\ \\frac{11}{38} \\\\ \\frac{5}{19}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6.8}{38}+\\frac{7.7}{38}+\\frac{1.25}{19} \\\\ \\frac{3.4}{38}+\\frac{1.1}{38}+\\frac{3.25}{19} \\\\ \\frac{6.8}{38}+\\frac{2.2}{38}+\\frac{0.5}{19}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{17}{38} \\\\ \\frac{11}{38} \\\\ \\frac{5}{19}\\end{aligned}]=q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector such that $Pq=q.$ So, it's a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Steady-State Vectors of Stochastic Matrices"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124616 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124427 :question/id #uuid "d8798f55-f005-5019-bf47-349e43237888" :question/math-academy-id "e-9179" :question/problem "The table below describes how the customers of a particular electronics store feel about their next computer purchase.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Laptop** | **Desktop** |\n| To: | **Laptop** | $0.9$ | $0.8$ |\n| **Desktop** | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |  |\n\nIt is found that $10 \\%$ of customers who currently use a laptop would choose a desktop on their next purchase, while $80 \\%$ of customers who currently use a desktop would choose a laptop on their next purchase. \n\nGiven that the store has $5\\,400$ customers, all of whom purchase a new computer every two years, how many customers would eventually own a desktop computer if the given probabilities remain constant over many two-year periods?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n![](images/19/1935c4960a48e2b446c2610b9edc2a160a5f45462494c72a90f139ea2d13cc34.png)\n\nNotice that $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many two-year periods, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n![](images/29/29b5a209bb27b24f165ee418326763d8fb24f83f2b3a767052c381aa8cd0e01c.png)\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(P-I)\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{0}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.1 & 0.8 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & -0.8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-0.1 & 0.8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=8{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n![](images/0e/0e8f620c3392dbb77b1353184e38eaf48b526aa82c502772a5582bd20c7f5aea.png)\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ 9{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur steady-state vector is\n![](images/2e/2ed7b0fc7296b2b651909c7c15c73e253fc8a9f95fb037072228d5de6f838894.png)\n\nSo, ${\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{1}{9}}}$ of the $5\\,400$ customers would eventually own a desktop computer. Therefore, the total number of people who would eventually own a desktop computer is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{9} \\cdot 5\\,400 = 600.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f53fa522-6d50-51d9-abdd-62735575a2d1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124772 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "245874df-8c61-5865-89e0-2eea58d0ddb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244636} {:answer/id #uuid "89ef273f-1ef4-5830-8401-02da27fbfef8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245524} {:answer/id #uuid "8f0491b5-2aa5-5777-aa10-2469c7c918ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245561} {:answer/id #uuid "b33e12af-527b-59a1-a8b0-87c0b7d8c14d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245909} {:answer/id #uuid "d997fab9-f7e0-5e91-9d3c-7082d52b45a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245524} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c0a3814-685f-5c2a-a989-456250f0e6e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246791}] :question/id #uuid "496d0736-5684-529d-8dd0-8b061c9a6089" :question/math-academy-id "q-110242" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.9\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P,$ what is the value of $a-b?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.9\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.6 & -0.1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0.6 & -0.1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{7}{6}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{6}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{6}{7}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=\\frac{1}{7}-\\frac{6}{7}=-\\frac{5}{7}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124774 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94f53f7e-0acf-53ad-bb28-7cfb9124883a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121242} {:answer/id #uuid "b48fbcca-07de-5b03-88a9-e02fa0e16d53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121243} {:answer/id #uuid "cf01b819-643f-5aa3-b9a8-ebd9af5e2416" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121244} {:answer/id #uuid "18f84519-38c2-5381-b81a-655b79d8dca8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8.5$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121245} {:answer/id #uuid "1e7044dc-c180-54a0-8f95-b3c46d0b640d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.5$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121246}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5a0f6e5f-6571-5dc2-aafe-8dc89196cf8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121241} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33a2da09-ade9-5f02-bc30-6ec862b4c502" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399728} {:answer/id #uuid "1f57ef16-daf6-55cf-b3cb-f0b639c205a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399729} {:answer/id #uuid "d2cdee49-7ce2-5cce-acee-cd378fd6b19d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8.5$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399730} {:answer/id #uuid "4c833b2d-9013-504c-b338-e5d0dc7d7a90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399731} {:answer/id #uuid "11a1059c-7f59-5f04-b1af-4e76b82a2739" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.5$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399732}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399728} :answer-field/id #uuid "6866c1e5-6616-5f78-9eb2-94b16182edd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399727}] :question/id #uuid "57c92fa7-df07-5594-a6b7-bd705bfe80a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-111172" :question/problem "The migration table below describes the movement of the population between cities and suburbs every year in a certain state. \n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Cities** | **Suburbs** |\n| To: | **Cities** | $0.8$ | $0.7$ |\n| **Suburbs** | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |  |\n\nThat is, $20%$ of those living in cities move to the suburbs each year, and $70%$ of those living in suburbs move to the cities.\n\nAssuming that the population of the state is $9$ million and remains constant, how many people would eventually live in cities if the given probabilities remain constant over many years?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 0.7 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.7 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 0.7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{7}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{2}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{9} \\\\ \\frac{2}{9}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{7}{9}$ of the $9$ million population will live in cities. Therefore, the total number of people living in cities after many years will be\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{9}\\cdot 9\\,\\text{million}=7\\,\\text{million}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269030 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b828146-3af8-5909-903e-b51a9b905309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266379} {:answer/id #uuid "783f6a21-5d08-51b0-a129-e4614e791183" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267200} {:answer/id #uuid "8d08161d-17c7-50f2-91f4-495659a8a63b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267404} {:answer/id #uuid "f8ca4cde-be3a-5390-b261-7492120df2ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268349} {:answer/id #uuid "fe1eb026-f8a8-5839-b850-5db4012183c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268349} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e83eae8-e4dc-5643-b310-0c6ec7afc244" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268487}] :question/id #uuid "d5136e32-dc7f-55de-b02b-4d944cd2c122" :question/math-academy-id "q-135323" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.25 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.75 & 0.4\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P,$ what is the value of $a-b?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.25 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.75 & 0.4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.75 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.75 & -0.6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.75 & 0.6 & 0 \\\\ 0.75 & -0.6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-0.75 & 0.6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-0.75{x}_{1}+0.6{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 75{x}_{1}-60{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{5}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ \\frac{5}{9}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=\\frac{4}{9}-\\frac{5}{9}=-\\frac{1}{9}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269031 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44403a26-1334-5d42-b45c-e6dc99cd5360" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266754} {:answer/id #uuid "833d8948-1095-57a4-8175-583255cf6c8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267300} {:answer/id #uuid "9d96fefb-ac81-5570-be7b-ad3724420674" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267562} {:answer/id #uuid "bf7b149d-26e3-5c42-8958-3f78e54b7596" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267860} {:answer/id #uuid "f7287a30-ffd4-55a3-b5aa-fa600c3c2e50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267562} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4b05734-aa03-5130-a34c-38dfff1a430e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268834}] :question/id #uuid "2907c394-03b6-5c50-b20f-3b387f7ffb42" :question/math-academy-id "q-135324" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 & 0.9 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.1\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P,$ what is the value of $a-b?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 & 0.9 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.5 & 0.9 \\\\ 0.5 & -0.9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.5 & 0.9 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & -0.9 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-0.5 & 0.9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-0.5{x}_{1}+0.9{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =\\frac{9}{5}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{9}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{5}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{14}{5}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{9}{14} \\\\ \\frac{5}{14}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=\\frac{9}{14}-\\frac{5}{14}=\\frac{2}{7}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269032 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19e0631e-07fa-5259-8791-2c5926d4ae5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266359} {:answer/id #uuid "5686887c-bf1f-5eef-8d6c-4c47be372a18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266913} {:answer/id #uuid "89d0ef52-9998-5e57-ac2e-1c6860957c1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267370} {:answer/id #uuid "8f0a0335-537e-50c2-9b17-5c27afb509f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267419} {:answer/id #uuid "9726d41f-9c36-579a-af0e-92267c382c99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267496} :answer-field/id #uuid "15f90970-79cf-5206-a259-f9ec67ec053f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268442}] :question/id #uuid "1e24f728-ce2c-55b9-a28e-e90dcc5c4465" :question/math-academy-id "q-135325" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0.3 \\\\ 1 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P,$ what is the value of $a-b?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0.3 \\\\ 1 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0.3 \\\\ 1 & -0.3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0.3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -0.3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0.3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+0.3{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 10{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =\\frac{3}{10}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{10}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{10}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{13}{10}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{10}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{13} \\\\ \\frac{10}{13}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=\\frac{3}{13}-\\frac{10}{13}=-\\frac{7}{13}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269034 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20a79a89-ec4c-5742-b6df-6f8c3a2f4bf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266435} {:answer/id #uuid "2824f8e2-cf3e-555c-9e5e-4f31a42cb6be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{33}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266501} {:answer/id #uuid "2ad4ecaa-1ae2-59b9-9202-1502ddbdc7a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266519} {:answer/id #uuid "5b295ee8-9d53-5f1d-ba28-925fa4b34b1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266950} {:answer/id #uuid "6bf69089-587b-5867-8786-9f622d37f7c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{33}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{33}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267097} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a5aeaa3-0e8a-54ce-bc2c-b0e772876eab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268629}] :question/id #uuid "3714772a-f74b-578a-a32c-f0e6e23862fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-135330" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.35 & 1 \\\\ 0.65 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P,$ what is the value of $a-b?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.35 & 1 \\\\ 0.65 & 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.65 & 1 \\\\ 0.65 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.65 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0.65 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-0.65 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-0.65{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 65{x}_{1}-100{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 13{x}_{1}-20{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =\\frac{20}{13}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{20}{13}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{20}{13}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{33}{13}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{13}{33}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{20}{33} \\\\ \\frac{13}{33}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=\\frac{20}{33}-\\frac{13}{33}=\\frac{7}{33}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269035 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8759a573-7553-5834-adc7-bb7ecebc071a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267341} {:answer/id #uuid "8f0e5979-4ac9-5e3d-9de3-365e6465dfa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267420} {:answer/id #uuid "96650908-cc82-5cc1-a604-e073fc34db3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267486} {:answer/id #uuid "a4065787-991b-5abe-8f52-e0833ba0558a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267621} {:answer/id #uuid "fed65815-0129-53dc-b1d7-9c523cd86a61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268388} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ee78886-ce53-5ed1-a620-8c46b1165547" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268543}] :question/id #uuid "69190782-47c3-5351-a63c-037e5f837593" :question/math-academy-id "q-135332" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0.6 \\\\ 1 & 0.4\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P,$ what is the value of $a-b?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0.6 \\\\ 1 & 0.4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0.6 \\\\ 1 & -0.6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0.6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -0.6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0.6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+0.6{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{8}{5}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ \\frac{5}{8}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=\\frac{3}{8}-\\frac{5}{8}=-\\frac{1}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269055 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b8dc13b-822a-5064-adf7-c311b031b3c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266251} {:answer/id #uuid "52e9a89b-ca43-571e-b659-44ac64d0296e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266886} {:answer/id #uuid "55233626-c846-5d9c-afdc-9c54f0236088" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22\\,400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266905} {:answer/id #uuid "6abbc98b-5186-5d9b-a5e7-7d9ed95ae610" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\,300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267088} {:answer/id #uuid "7349d45f-c472-5776-903b-eb79dc1e34f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\,200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\,300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267088} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c6d9e4b-9348-5202-b8dc-eefd96bb1a5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268452}] :question/id #uuid "35bb1a67-ff7a-5da5-9b52-f94711e5bbed" :question/math-academy-id "q-135407" :question/problem "A town planning department wants to know how its citizens' preferences for traveling to work are changing. So they conducted a survey, and the results are shown below.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Car** | **Subway** |\n| To: | **Car** | $0.5$ | $0.6$ |\n| **Subway** | $0.5$ | $0.4$ |  |\n\nThey found that $50%$ of those surveyed that currently drive to work are planning to switch to the subway next year, while $60%$ of the people surveyed that currently use the subway are planning to switch to their cars next year.\n\nAssuming that the town has $50\\,000$ that travel to work by car or subway, and the given probabilities remain constant over many years, how many people will eventually use their cars to drive to work? Round your answer to the nearest hundreds." :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.5 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.5 & -0.6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.5 & 0.6 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & -0.6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-0.5 & 0.6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{6}{5}{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{6}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{5}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{11}{5}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{6}{11} \\\\ \\frac{5}{11}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{6}{11}$ of the $50\\,000$ people surveyed would use their cars. Therefore, the total number of people who will eventually use their cars is\n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{11}\\cdot 50\\,000≈27\\,300.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269056 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b7a7e5d-76cc-5a5b-8fc9-fc2d065c203d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267237} {:answer/id #uuid "7f20b487-b010-568a-a779-aeb7ba3871af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "62\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267263} {:answer/id #uuid "b6c78b2a-fdee-5ed0-8adf-6c7047ded625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267778} {:answer/id #uuid "e2323f2b-578f-5ade-a62f-5a863e325945" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268142} {:answer/id #uuid "e833fd16-0dbc-5db9-8c59-dd1eeea98194" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267237} :answer-field/id #uuid "88f9d971-672b-52c6-a33e-21c8b357ea30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268650}] :question/id #uuid "a2ead374-20fc-5336-8f26-ef768edbfcce" :question/math-academy-id "q-135408" :question/problem "There are two groups of people In a small town: those who travel to work by car and those who travel by train. Every year a study is done in which people are asked about their preference between using trains versus cars. The table below shows the results of this study.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Car** | **Train** |\n| To: | **Car** | $0.35$ | $0.25$ |\n| **Train** | $0.65$ | $0.75$ |  |\n\nIt's found that $65%$ of people who currently travel by car will switch to train next year, while $25%$ of people who currently travel by train will switch to their car next year.\n\nAssuming that the town has $90\\,000$ people that travel to work by train or car, and the given probabilities remain constant over many years, how many people will eventually use the train to travel to work?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.35 & 0.25 \\\\ 0.65 & 0.75\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.35 & 0.25 \\\\ 0.65 & 0.75\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.65 & 0.25 \\\\ 0.65 & -0.25\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.65 & 0.25 & 0 \\\\ 0.65 & -0.25 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-0.65 & 0.25 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{5}{13}{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{13}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{13}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{18}{13}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{13}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{18} \\\\ \\frac{13}{18}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{13}{18}$ of the people will eventually use the train to travel. Therefore, the number of people who will eventually use the train is\n\n$$\n\\frac{13}{18}\\cdot 90\\,000=65\\,000.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269057 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ba600a6-1907-5e14-b550-b044551ef5e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266530} {:answer/id #uuid "33aab18e-ac2a-5fe2-8c2c-6bbd3d17578b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "85\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266597} {:answer/id #uuid "656ef5ce-de1c-585b-b58e-bcb0488a1f4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267042} {:answer/id #uuid "78a20fcd-e709-5231-beab-5b7abd75b2d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267205} {:answer/id #uuid "84bfcca2-04fa-5a72-b5ec-b6d4efc25021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267313} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e7c105c-3db3-51ac-83dc-0a87716a4e48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268607}] :question/id #uuid "b9da89ba-6ad8-53a7-ac87-9c6b7aab9eb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-135409" :question/problem "A factory produces two types of sugar: brown and extra-refined. Every year, the factory's sales executives study how customers' preferences change between the two types of sugar. The table below shows the results of this study.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Brown** | **Extra-Refined** |\n| To: | **Brown** | $0.8$ | $1$ |\n| **Extra-Refined** | $0.2$ | $0$ |  |\n\nIt's found that $20%$ of customers who currently buy brown sugar will switch to extra-refined sugar next year, while $100%$ of customers who currently purchase extra-refined sugar will switch to brown sugar next year.\n\nAssuming that there are $450\\,000$ customers in total and that the given probabilities remain constant over many years, how many customers will eventually consume extra-refined sugar?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 1 \\\\ 0.2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n{P}^{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.84 & 0.8 \\\\ 0.16 & 0.2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${P}^{2}$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 1 \\\\ 0.2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 1 \\\\ 0.2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=5{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ 6{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{6} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the customers will eventually use extra-refined sugar. Therefore, the number of customers who will eventually use this type of sugar is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 450\\,000=75\\,000.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411617 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d26e28b-a37c-5f8e-b505-641bddc3ac1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "82\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411598} {:answer/id #uuid "13e7a544-2b8e-535c-9dbd-150b4b2182d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411599} {:answer/id #uuid "62af4b1d-dead-5e6e-8055-bd94bce27217" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411605} {:answer/id #uuid "8d2be5c6-2ea1-5fdd-a19b-1ca6e9db49af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411608} {:answer/id #uuid "c2f0e3cc-f79a-5b7f-a8f4-e19e89af1d2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411609} :answer-field/id #uuid "f22ac3a1-3b1b-5c02-9aac-e44227bf9c37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411614}] :question/id #uuid "fbc10db1-83ee-5f40-90d1-b581f73d6e92" :question/math-academy-id "q-135412" :question/problem "A study determines how customers' yearly preferences change between the LB and SB telephone companies. The table below describes the results of this study.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **LB** | **SB** |\n| To: | **LB** | $0$ | $0.25$ |\n| **SB** | $1$ | $0.75$ |  |\n\nIt's found that $100%$ of the customers who currently use the LB company will choose the SB company next year, while $25%$ of the customers who currently use the SB company will use the LB company next year.\n\nAssuming that there are $100\\,000$ customers in total and that the given probabilities remain constant over many years, determine the number of customers who will eventually use the SB company." :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0.25 \\\\ 1 & 0.75\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n{P}^{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.25 & 0.187\\,5 \\\\ 0.75 & 0.812\\,5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${P}^{2}$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0.25 \\\\ 1 & 0.75\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0.25 \\\\ 1 & -0.25\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0.25 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -0.25 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0.25 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{4}{5}$ of the customers will eventually use the SB company. Therefore, the number of customers who will eventually use this company is\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot 100\\,000=80\\,000.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 2x2 Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124617 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124428 :question/id #uuid "2164d0ba-43d5-53c7-adf1-9e6810b316a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9180" :question/problem "![](images/09/093de94431946cb83e7cc0523405989b7a23cc8d9677bdecdfba7aaf207fd62b.png)\n\nFind the steady-state vector $\\mathbf q$ of the stochastic matrix $P$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n![](images/5e/5ed2f76d9b2857134a5b2aa5781711f9625cabb46a9ca3c3d54aa31c32bb4dbe.png)\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{0}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.9 & 0.3 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.7 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.4 & -0.6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=20{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=20{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-18 & 6 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 14 & 8 & -12 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -18 & 6 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 14 & 8 & -12 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+9{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-7{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -57 & 19 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 57 & -19 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -57 & 19 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\nx_2=\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n2x_1 -7\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3\\right) + x_3 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_1 = \\dfrac{2}{3}x_3.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n![](images/9a/9ae5533dc825d2d3d08ca7a54c50578e21dda8fad7988b9043baaad2a69368b7.png)\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 2{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, our steady-state vector is\n![](images/fd/fd7775b5d982d0bc51e80417a70b67dbff0176afcc5ff9aec613947076201386.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84798595-cdd4-57bf-953f-0856b041b65f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124773 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "864ade46-01a0-5dbe-aaeb-70b080238c2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121236} {:answer/id #uuid "91c60bcf-63de-5061-a278-e0de92932ce7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121237} {:answer/id #uuid "92954c49-8e23-52ad-a533-209d6f104703" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121238} {:answer/id #uuid "9a7fa6cd-38ab-5343-80a2-d83135dd32a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121239} {:answer/id #uuid "0ff76532-db6a-5ad9-bd52-d3ab3ac2e98a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121240}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9b3ab77a-f24c-5f05-9941-6e64bc0c5347" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121235}] :question/id #uuid "9d9e86d2-c897-5b07-851a-148dd18a6f1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-110243" :question/problem "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}0.9 & 0.2 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & 0.1 \\\\ 0 & 0.6 & 0.6\\end{bmatrix}, q = \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P$ shown above, what is the value of $a + b - c$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124775 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "327c20aa-5b6d-5d5d-95d5-67fe1edd6dda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244765} {:answer/id #uuid "33ffc812-4891-586d-bc0d-281bd530059e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244781} {:answer/id #uuid "be00ba0f-33e3-51bb-b788-c863cec54aad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246007} {:answer/id #uuid "beae8b6f-7eef-5f2b-b111-68f42f84b6fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246016} {:answer/id #uuid "e6a13eed-4a8b-5532-9d37-57dbd1d42511" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "480" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246016} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb514e9d-3fa7-5bab-af7e-44674ca75bc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246913}] :question/id #uuid "cac3744a-0cdf-56d6-88ab-9bba2eefa254" :question/math-academy-id "q-111192" :question/problem "A particular town has a fleet of $720$ bicycles for rent. The table below gives the pattern of rental and return locations over a typical day. \n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Museum** | **Station** | **Gym** |\n| To: | **Museum** | $0.9$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ |\n| **Station** | $0$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ |  |\n| **Gym** | $0.1$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ |  |\n\nFor example, $40%$ of the bicycles at the Station are moved to the Gym every day.\n\nHow many bicycles would eventually be located at the Station if the listed probabilities remained constant over many days?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.9 & 0.3 & 0.1 \\\\ 0 & 0.3 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.4 & 0.2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix ${P}^{2}$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. \nAs a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.9 & 0.3 & 0.1 \\\\ 0 & 0.3 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.4 & 0.2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.1 & 0.3 & 0.1 \\\\ 0 & -0.7 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.4 & -0.8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.1 & 0.3 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -0.7 & 0.7 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.4 & -0.8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=10{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -7 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-{x}_{1}+3({x}_{3})+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=4{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 6{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, after many days, $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the bicycles will be at the station. Therefore, the total number of bikes at the station after many days will be\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 720=120.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269036 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b373c41-8b42-50f5-90d0-b51a7c83360c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266524} {:answer/id #uuid "d011fc48-45b4-58ae-99ed-0034c009ba75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268004} {:answer/id #uuid "ddb9f8b1-5a19-57d1-ab57-3438a2df3158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268101} {:answer/id #uuid "f28e16fb-1222-5743-b296-45c4f2b1221e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268300} {:answer/id #uuid "ff283d97-948c-5367-8a78-e4d3b3cf4c4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268300} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcddcb02-38f8-5b6f-bc4a-2daf7cc42e53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268744}] :question/id #uuid "26bfb2a9-4e8e-55d5-877c-2b96948f29b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-135344" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.2 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.2 & 0.65 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.6 & 0.25\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P$ shown above, what is the value of $a+b-c?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.2 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.2 & 0.65 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.6 & 0.25\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.2 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.3 & -0.8 & 0.65 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.6 & -0.75\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.2 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0.3 & -0.8 & 0.65 & 0 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.6 & -0.75 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=5{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -8 & 6.5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6 & -7.5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -7 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-3{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}+0.5{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b-c=\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269037 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08c0c7e1-e02b-5682-ac80-d3a94019c309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266223} {:answer/id #uuid "11fb7365-d701-5402-9321-e9bcfadc7e90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266294} {:answer/id #uuid "124a7b4d-ae77-57dc-9cf2-950cf7d343b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266301} {:answer/id #uuid "d27f584c-dc81-5acc-8b77-a22d6fce775d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268025} {:answer/id #uuid "f43fe715-3933-563a-88bf-aba043805e87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268313} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8c27985-ba86-55fb-959f-022ac3234d3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268847}] :question/id #uuid "4852783a-33d0-5412-afc5-cce71dd3a6d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-135345" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 & 0.3 & 0 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.5 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & 0.65\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P$ shown above, what is the value of $a+b-c?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 & 0.3 & 0 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.5 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & 0.65\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.9 & 0.3 & 0 \\\\ 0.8 & -0.5 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & -0.35\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.9 & 0.3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0.8 & -0.5 & 0.35 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & -0.35 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=10{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -5 & 3.5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3.5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3.5 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -5 & 3.5 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 3 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-8{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+9{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -21 & 31.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 21 & -31.5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -21 & 31.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{31.5}{21}{x}_{3}=\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3})-3.5{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 3{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b-c=\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269039 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4eb2fe76-32b7-514f-bb2e-89698a403db5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266845} {:answer/id #uuid "5fd5a800-a03a-5e01-ba96-0ac9151231ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266990} {:answer/id #uuid "a117550e-deb7-5fd6-bd07-a00068e6e499" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267596} {:answer/id #uuid "b94b7abd-5692-581d-9a3c-f648ded28ca4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267806} {:answer/id #uuid "c487e502-1285-5b73-b57b-667e8d4dcbac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267806} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4613a71-c841-55fc-9277-7af5997d67da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268727}] :question/id #uuid "523b5c51-5d69-5337-be0d-a6c9d40f6678" :question/math-academy-id "q-135352" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & 0.4\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P$ shown above, what is the value of $a+b-c?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & 0.4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.6 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & -0.6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.4 & 0.4 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.6 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & -0.6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=10{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10 & -14 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{14}{10}{x}_{3}=\\frac{7}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}+2(\\frac{7}{5}{x}_{3})-6{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{8}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{8}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{7}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{5}{x}_{3}+\\frac{7}{5}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{20}{5}{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{7}{20} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b-c=\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{7}{20}-\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{10}{20}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269041 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c0a51b1-3fb3-532b-9bce-810bc81de041" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266536} {:answer/id #uuid "5da4c788-5786-5fb3-83c4-5d05b69e57ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266971} {:answer/id #uuid "7b453679-36a2-5eec-bf15-793d045294a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267233} {:answer/id #uuid "d19580ed-ed50-52b3-b155-6355c947c140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268019} {:answer/id #uuid "da129d77-bd1b-5e0d-b680-a13aae3611b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267233} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef910a2a-837b-5d26-96fe-ad3445b5dcb2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268827}] :question/id #uuid "7ff5c6dd-1891-5f28-95c5-0a779435ce27" :question/math-academy-id "q-135355" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.6 & 0.9 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & 0.1\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P$ shown above, what is the value of $a+b-c?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.6 & 0.9 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & 0.1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.6 & 0.9 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.8 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & -0.9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.6 & 0.9 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & -0.9 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=10{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 6 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -9 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -12 & 18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 12 & -18 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -12 & 18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-6{x}_{1}+6(\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3})+9{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=3{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{3}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{11}{2}{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{2}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{6}{11} \\\\ \\frac{3}{11} \\\\ \\frac{2}{11}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b-c=\\frac{6}{11}+\\frac{3}{11}-\\frac{2}{11}=\\frac{7}{11}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269059 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4091d3e1-ecb5-5bc3-9900-0e7e79204ea2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266728} {:answer/id #uuid "64dbfb07-b357-5a4a-b63f-99a0ceea8dbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267037} {:answer/id #uuid "7f2e03c7-a610-58c5-82fc-3c96c1da9746" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267264} {:answer/id #uuid "96714f20-7595-51a3-b5a1-3fd3eff17fee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267487} {:answer/id #uuid "c3162ec5-9335-5008-a73e-e6c4571c9174" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267487} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e74ea9d-86a9-5cfd-90d9-6e3b8e013280" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268606}] :question/id #uuid "4d1e3f01-76a8-5dc2-a5d5-825db1b3cf60" :question/math-academy-id "q-135432" :question/problem "A company processes and packages three types of rice: rice $A,$ rice $B,$ and rice $C.$ A study by the company's sales analysts demonstrates how consumer preference changes from one kind of rice to another year after year. The table below shows the results of this study.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Rice $A$** | **Rice $B$** | **Rice $C$** |\n| To: | **Rice $A$** | $0.5$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ |\n| **Rice $B$** | $0.2$ | $0.7$ | $0.2$ |  |\n| **Rice $C$** | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.65$ |  |\n\nFor example, $20%$ of the people who preferred rice $B$ last year switched to rice $C$ this year.\n\nIf the company has $200\\,000$ customers and the listed probabilities remain constant over many years, how many customers will eventually purchase rice $B?$" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 & 0.1 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.7 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.2 & 0.65\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. \nAs a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 & 0.1 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.7 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.2 & 0.65\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.5 & 0.1 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.3 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.2 & -0.35\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.5 & 0.1 & 0.15 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.3 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.2 & -0.35 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=10{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=100{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=100{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 & 1.5 & 0 \\\\ 20 & -30 & 20 & 0 \\\\ 30 & 20 & -35 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+6{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 & 1.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -26 & 26 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 26 & -26 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 & 1.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -26 & 26 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-5{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{2}{5}$ of the customers will consume rice $B.$ Therefore, the number of people who will eventually consume rice $B$ will be\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot 200\\,000=80\\,000.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269060 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08abe10c-612c-5ef8-9d55-c8b91569cd10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1.6$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266222} {:answer/id #uuid "09d32f2f-0057-55d3-bb07-35ca54ac43c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1.2$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266232} {:answer/id #uuid "67ac7dca-cb0f-59bd-ab0f-d1f00c7bab50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.8$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267063} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f6aa0c-b3dd-57a3-af3e-9de99d2f6aa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267619} {:answer/id #uuid "f8266544-a5e1-5f13-85cd-a874b53d51ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.9$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.8$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267063} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca8e28ea-ed10-593b-a9c2-93477c01f17b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268769}] :question/id #uuid "fabc74b8-a791-5627-801a-2888d8f737ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-135433" :question/problem "A study has recorded and analyzed the results of the parliamentary elections of a particular country, held every four years. The study shows how voter preference changes from one political party to another. The table below shows the results of this study.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Yellow Party** | **Blue Party** | **Red Party** |\n| To: | **Yellow Party** | $0.7$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n| **Blue Party** | $0.2$ | $0.8$ | $0.3$ |  |\n| **Red Party** | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ |  |\n\nFor example, $10%$ of the people who voted for the yellow party in the last election will vote for the red party in the next election.\n\nIf the electorate contains $5.6$ million voters and the given probabilities remain constant election after election, how many people will vote for the red party after many elections have taken place?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.7 & 0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.8 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & 0.4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. \nAs a result, after many elections, the distribution will eventually converge to the steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.7 & 0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.8 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & 0.4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.3 & 0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.2 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & -0.6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.3 & 0.1 & 0.3 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.2 & 0.3 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & -0.6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=10{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -15 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{15}{4}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+\\frac{15}{4}{x}_{3}-6{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{9}{4}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{9}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{15}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4}{x}_{3}+\\frac{15}{4}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 7{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{9}{28} \\\\ \\frac{15}{28} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, after many elections, $\\frac{1}{7}$ of the people will vote for the red party. Therefore, the number of people that will vote for the red party equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot 5.6\\,\\text{million}=0.8\\,\\text{million}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272188 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "157f5ca9-b81c-53c8-b1d3-29be34acd7e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269453} {:answer/id #uuid "21680cff-4228-5c00-9484-24e5b719268c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "540" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269552} {:answer/id #uuid "3728440d-e1a2-5207-95ac-9416bd057cf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "720" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269768} {:answer/id #uuid "a163dbe7-d913-56ca-a615-fbf7e9977ea8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "630" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270700} {:answer/id #uuid "d28a917b-3f74-5562-b106-c78d82823886" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271123}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271123} :answer-field/id #uuid "264e87e8-2ef0-5cf0-82c6-75d0173c6ac2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271579}] :question/id #uuid "03023a20-ec6d-5cf1-9ba8-6fa6f21acb01" :question/math-academy-id "q-141035" :question/problem "A study shows how the sports preferences of young people in a certain community vary from year to year. The following table shows the results of this study. \n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Baseball** | **Basketball** | **Football** |\n| To: | **Baseball** | $0.8$ | $0.2$ | $0$ |\n| **Basketball** | $0.1$ | $0.6$ | $0.2$ |  |\n| **Football** | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ |  |\n\nFor example, $10%$ of people who used to prefer baseball now prefer football. \n\nGiven that $1\\,260$ young people were interviewed for the study and that the probabilities listed remain constant for many years, how many of those people will eventually prefer baseball?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.6 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & 0.8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix ${P}^{2}$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. \nAs a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.6 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & 0.8\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & -0.4 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & -0.2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & -0.4 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & -0.2\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=-5{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}+0{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{7}{3}{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{3}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ \\frac{2}{7} \\\\ \\frac{3}{7}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{2}{7}$ of the people will prefer baseball. Therefore, the number of people who will prefer baseball is\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{7}\\cdot 1\\,260=360\\,\\text{people}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388820 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aea59a55-ca8c-5011-94bb-adc5e2783f76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388822} {:answer/id #uuid "8e324f12-a685-504b-be59-f46dd3f2728f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "340" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388823} {:answer/id #uuid "02091894-fa17-5170-9a72-9c82ed0e6bbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "680" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388824} {:answer/id #uuid "d0746dbd-50e5-51db-b5aa-9a4c741c97d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,040" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388825} {:answer/id #uuid "a69c66c2-72df-5f5b-aab3-269b3148afdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,700" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,700" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388826} :answer-field/id #uuid "c17d838d-64b9-5408-9e52-5f894639d0cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388821}] :question/id #uuid "a031dbeb-978c-5542-b4e9-e4e60f2dc984" :question/math-academy-id "q-141024" :question/problem "A study conducted on $3\\,400$ teenagers by the transportation department of a particular city reveals that teenagers' preference for the type of home-to-school transportation changes with time. The following table shows the results of this study.  \n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Bus** | **Bicycle** | **Electric Scooter ** |\n| To: | **Bus** | $0.2$ | $0.2$ | $0$ |\n| **Bicycle** | $0.4$ | $0.4$ | $0.4$ |  |\n| **Electric Scooter** | $0.4$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ |  |\n\nFor example, $40%$ of teenagers who used to go to school by bus now go by bicycle.  \n\nHow many teenagers would eventually go to school on electric scooters if the listed probabilities remained constant over many years?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix ${P}^{2}$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. \nAs a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.6\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.8 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.6 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & -0.4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.8 & 0.2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.6 & 0.4 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & -0.4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=5{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=5{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3})-{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3}+\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 2{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ \\frac{4}{10} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{1}{2}$ of the teenagers will go to school in electric scooters. Therefore, the total number of teenagers in the city that will go to school in electric scooters will be\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\,400=1\\,700.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411615 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09fa980f-8d16-5c0b-adc6-32aaa0351649" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411597} {:answer/id #uuid "3f726a36-e309-5b98-8554-2e43c91e6dd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411601} {:answer/id #uuid "47f65641-4908-5c8e-b21e-1a448edd51ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411602} {:answer/id #uuid "49e05de2-77df-5db3-a385-91d68a9be627" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411603} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d1b46f-e211-5172-be37-ae8796943205" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411601} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e744f0f-1893-571c-a245-886e0be7796d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411612}] :question/id #uuid "15361c81-aace-5a26-b7c5-2e82d38f451f" :question/math-academy-id "q-135349" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 & 0.4 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.5 & 0.2\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P$ shown above, what is the value of $a+b-c?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 & 0.4 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.5 & 0.2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.9 & 0.4 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.6 & -0.9 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.5 & -0.8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.9 & 0.4 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0.6 & -0.9 & 0.3 & 0 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.5 & -0.8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=10{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -9 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -9 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 4 & 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -19 & 19 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 19 & -19 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -19 & 19 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n3{x}_{1}+5{x}_{3}-8{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}={x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 3{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b-c=\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 3x3 Matrix"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1913 :topic/title "Steady-State Vectors"}]}
